  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In the figure, a very large plane sheet of positive charge is shown. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are two points at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></math> from the charge distribution. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#963;</mi></math> is the surface charge density, then the magnitude of electric fields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively are&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/d58dc51f-2638-4746-b21d-a8ee4186aa11-image.png\" style=\"width: 230px; height: 160px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "a95Fp",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>&#963;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>&#963;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>",
